    "description": "Private hospitals form part of Iran’s mixed system of hospital provision alongside public and charitable facilities. Understanding their role requires separating ownership, insurance financing, geographic access and evidence about service quality.",
    "tags": ["Private hospitals", "Hospital services", "Patient experience", "Health insurance"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:57:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:57:09Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Private Hospitals in Iran: Access, Payment and Patient Experience",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to private hospitals in Iran, including financing distinctions and the limits of patient-experience research."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mixed-provision",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A distinct part of hospital provision",
        "content": "Private hospitals belong to Iran’s wider private health sector, which also includes independent practices, diagnostic services and other providers. Evidence about this entire sector should not automatically be interpreted as evidence about inpatient hospitals alone. The Commonwealth Fund describes private providers as operating under licensing and regulation involving the Ministry of Health and Medical Education and its affiliated institutions. [s1]\n\nOwnership also needs to be distinguished from financing. The profile describes public insurance purchasing some services from private providers, alongside direct patient payments and supplementary insurance. A privately operated facility can therefore receive payments from different sources. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "patient-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What patient research can establish",
        "content": "An Iranian study published in February 2012 examined patients’ expectations and experiences in private general hospitals in Tehran. It used SERVQUAL, a questionnaire approach that compares expected service with perceived service. The research found that patients’ reported experiences fell below expectations and highlighted responsiveness and empathy as areas needing attention. [s2]\n\nThe study usefully separates technical quality from functional quality. Technical quality concerns the accuracy of diagnosis and treatment; functional quality concerns how care is delivered and experienced. Patients can report whether staff communicate clearly or respond to their needs, but those reports alone cannot establish the clinical effectiveness of treatment. [s2]\n\nIts findings therefore support a limited conclusion: private ownership does not remove the need to examine communication, trust and individual attention. They do not establish present-day performance, a national ranking or superiority over public hospitals. The research concerned selected Tehran facilities and a historical survey period. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geography-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Geography and the limits of national claims",
        "content": "The World Health Organization’s regional assessment describes a tendency for private for-profit facilities to concentrate in urban areas with greater purchasing power. Its discussion identifies concentration in Tehran as an issue for Iran. This helps explain why national private-sector capacity and local access are different questions: the presence of facilities in a major city does not establish comparable availability elsewhere. [s3]\n\nThe assessment relates this urban pattern to demand, infrastructure and investment conditions. These are structural explanations for provider location, rather than evidence that a particular hospital refuses patients or delivers better care. Its findings also should not be read as a current map of operational hospitals. [s3]\n\nTaken together, the inspected evidence suggests separate questions for understanding private hospital provision: where facilities operate, how care is paid for, what patients experience and what clinical outcomes are documented. Available research answers parts of this picture, but does not justify a single nationwide judgment about quality or affordability. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
